<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
<head>
  <title>ASCIIIMG:ASCII to MiraMon raster file conversion</title>
<meta content="text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1" http-equiv="Content-Type"/></head>

<body>
  <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<h1><span id="wpstitol"/></h1>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->
<p>This program converts raster files from several ASCII formats to MiraMon (IMG format). Supported ASCII formats may have header records (lines). These lines normally refer to the geometrical characteristics of the file. Depending on the treatment of these header lines, ASCIIIMG has three options:</p>
<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->
  <p>Thereby, ASCIIIMG supports four main types of ASCII files:</p>
  <ul>
  <li><a href="http://resources.esri.com/help/9.3/arcgisengine/java/GP_ToolRef/spatial_analyst_tools/esri_ascii_raster_format.htm?"> ESRI ASCII raster format</a>: The ArcGIS format ASCII (normally with .asc extension) is imported.
    <br/>
	 <br/>
    </li>
    <li>Fixed record: In this case the values of each cell or pixel of the raster always occupy the same number of characters in the file. Consequently the values can even be "pasted" together.
Examples: <br/>
     Examples: 
      <pre>
     -&gt; &quot; 45 73  2 12&quot;
</pre>contains, on a fixed record of 3 characters, the values 45, 73, 2 and 12.  
      <pre>
     -&gt; &quot;123 45  7255&quot;
</pre>contains, on a fixed record of 3 characters, the values 123, 45, 7 and 255.<br/>

      <br/>
      In this type carriage returns are ignored.<br/>
      <br/>
    </li>
    <li>Values separated by a delimiter. The delimiter can consist on any number of blanks, tabs or carriage returns.<br/>
      Examples: 
      <pre>
     -&gt; 45 73 2 12
     -&gt; 45      73 2 12
     -&gt; 45
        73
        2
        12
</pre>
 It is the most frequent case.
    </li><br/>
    <li>Columns structured values.<br/>
      Example:
      <pre>
    V    68    Matorral
    V    84    Coníferas
    A    37    Agua
    V    72    Matorral
    </pre>          
    </li>     
  </ul>

  <p>Supported output formats are: bit, byte, integer, uinteger, long and real.</p>

  <p>ASCIIIMG supports carriage returns in DOS-style (0x0D 0x0A) and UNIX-style (0x0D).</p>

  <p>A flag value can be indicated. This value will be used when the conversion is out of range.</p>

  <p>The program documents the fields:</p>
  <pre>
data type   : 
file type   : 
columns     : 
rows        : 
min. value  : 
max. value  : 
flag value  : 
</pre>If more arguments are provided in command line, it also documents:  
  <pre>
ref. system : 
ref. units  : 
min. X      : 
max. X      : 
min. Y      : 
max. Y      : 
resolution  : 
</pre>If this is not indicated, by default they will be documented as: 
  <pre>
ref. system : plane
ref. units  : pixels
min. X      : 0
max. X      : nfil
min. Y      : 0
max. Y      : ncol
resolution  : 1
</pre>The rest will be documented as: 
  <pre>
file title  : 
unit dist.  : 1.0
pos'n error : unknown
value units : unspecified
value error : unknown
flag def'n  : background
legend cats : 0
</pre>

  <p>The program controls whether the input image is complete or not (truncated). In the last case, if the deficit of pixels is less than a 70%, the program fills the rest of the image with the last read value. This way, the imported image can be visualized or clipped to keep only the valid cells.</p>
  <hr/>

  <exclusiva>
